{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-105711",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-G) & =∇\\times F-∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z & 0 & xy\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(0)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)]k \\\\  & =(x-0)i-(y-\\frac{1}{z})j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =x\\,i+(\\frac{1}{z}-y)j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-G) & =∇\\times F-∇\\times G \\\\  & =x\\,i+(\\frac{1}{z}-y)j-[-i+2z\\,j-x\\,k] \\\\  & =x\\,i+(\\frac{1}{z}-y)j+i-2z\\,j+x\\,k \\\\  & =(x+1)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{z}-y-2z)j+x\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(0,2,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F-G))(0,2,1) & =(0+1)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1}-2-2(1))j+0\\,k \\\\  & =i-3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
